Author a **looping waterfall**, a falling sheet of water that breaks into spray, as
a 3D particle **system** using only the particle tool.

It is a **steady-state** effect: the water is already falling and keeps falling,
with no start and no end. A sheet of blue droplets pours down from the top edge under
gravity, stretched along its velocity into thin streaks; where it reaches the base it
breaks into a billowing white spray that kicks up and fades; and a faint pale mist
drifts low. You are authoring the *effect* as a **system** that loops seamlessly over
its window — not a single frozen frame, and not a one-shot that fires and dies.

## How the particle tool works

The only way to shape the effect is the `particle-3d` binary already on your
`PATH`. It is the sole channel, and — this is the key idea — you do **not** place
individual particles. You **author a system**: emitters that spawn particles,
forces that push them around, and per-particle curves for size, opacity, and color
over each particle's life. The review UI and the game **simulate that system live**,
exactly the way a real particle editor (Unreal's Niagara, Unity's VFX Graph) plays
a system. Because it is a live stochastic simulation, the effect **varies slightly
from one play to the next** — so build something that reads as a waterfall across
replays and from any orbit angle, not a frozen arrangement.

You build the system by calling `particle-3d` **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**. A system produced any other way is
discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
particle-3d add-emitter --name fall --shape box --rate 200 --at-y 60 --lifetime 700 --speed 4 --dir-y -1
```

This effect is a **steady-state loop**: keep the timeline looping and emit at a
continuous **rate** (not a one-shot burst) so the fall and its spray never empty
out, and the tail of the window flows seamlessly back into its head. Rendering is a
separate, on-request step: run `particle-3d render` to **simulate the system over its
duration, write the preview animation, and emit the `system.json` your result is
built from**. An unrendered effect leaves an empty system, so you **must** run
`render` before you finish. The field dimensions, duration, and playback fps are
already seeded in a config next to your workspace, so no operation needs those flags.

Run `particle-3d --help` to list every operation and `particle-3d <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read the rendered `effect.gif` after your calls** to judge the
effect's read, motion, and seamless loop, and decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Author the waterfall system operation by operation — the falling water sheet, the
billowing base spray, and the faint drifting mist (as continuous emitters), with the
forces and per-particle curves that make the water accelerate down and stretch, the
spray billow out and fade, and the mist drift, and the timeline kept **looping** so
the effect never starts or ends — running `particle-3d render` to simulate and check
the effect as you go. When the simulated `effect.gif` reads as a continuous waterfall
that loops with no visible seam, run `render` one last time and stop: the recorded
`actions.json` (and the `system.json` it emits) is your finished submission.
